🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗿𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗕𝗲𝗰𝗮𝘂𝘀𝗲 𝗼𝗳 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗯𝗲 𝗙𝗮𝗶𝗹𝘂𝗿𝗲 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes application is running, but the pod keeps restarting because of failed health probes. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I wouldn't immediately remove the probe.

First, I would understand which probe is failing and why.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check pod status

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Check the READY status and restart count.

2️⃣ Describe the pod

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Check the Events section for messages such as:

🔹 Liveness probe failed
🔹 Readiness probe failed
🔹 Connection refused
🔹 Timeout
🔹 HTTP error

3️⃣ Identify the failing probe

Check the Deployment configuration:

kubectl get deployment <deployment-name> -o yaml

Review:

livenessProbe

readinessProbe

startupProbe


4️⃣ Test the health endpoint

If the probe uses an HTTP endpoint such as:

/health

I would test whether the application is actually responding on the expected port and path.

For example:

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- curl localhost:8080/health

5️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for slow startup, dependency failures, database connectivity, or application errors.

6️⃣ Review probe configuration

Check whether:

🔹 Port is correct
🔹 Path is correct
🔹 Initial delay is sufficient
🔹 Timeout is appropriate
🔹 Failure threshold is reasonable
🔹 Application startup time is considered

For slow-starting applications, a properly configured startupProbe can prevent premature liveness failures.

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Pod → Events → Probe Type → Endpoint/Port → Application Logs → Configuration → Root Cause

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A failed probe doesn't always mean the application is broken.

Sometimes the application is healthy but the probe configuration doesn't match the application's actual behavior.
